## Why This Is Asked

Beginner security tasks often miss logs containing PII. This question tests practical, actionable steps to prevent leakage without disrupting features.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation in logs
- PII redaction and masking
- Centralized, encrypted log sinks
- Least-privilege log access and audit trails
- CI validation for log content

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redactPII function for logs
function redactPII(record){
  const masked = {...record};
  if (masked.email) masked.email = masked.email.replace(/[^@]+@/,'***@');
  if (masked.phone) masked.phone = masked.phone.replace(/\d(?=\d{4})/g,'*');
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you integrate this into CI/CD and log shipping?
- How would you verify in production that no PII is emitted in logs?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical NoSQL injection awareness in a common Node.js + MongoDB setup, focusing on concrete defenses and verification within a tight window.

## Key Concepts

- NoSQL injection
- Input whitelisting
- Safe query construction
- MongoDB schema validation
- Query sanitization tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Safe query builder example (Express handler)
const allowedFields = ['title','author','year'];
function buildQuery(params){
  const q = {};
  for (const [k,v] of Object.entries(params)) {
    if (allowedFields.includes(k) && typeof v === 'string') q[k] = v;
  }
  if (Object.keys(params).some(k => k.startsWith('$') || k.includes('.'))) {
    throw new Error('Invalid query');
  }
  return q;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test NoSQL injection resilience in CI?
- How can you extend this to handle nested documents or arrays?
- What DB-level controls (RBAC, JSON schema validation) would you add for defense in depth?

